# Tailscale IPv6 kernel diagnosis — 2026-09-26

Status: diagnosis complete; live correction NOT activated. Phase 5 and SSH check-to-accept remain blocked by an unproven safe rollback and cross-interface effects.

## Confirmed evidence

The original network allow-all policy was fully restored on 2026-09-24 (see TAILNET_PHASE5_IPV6_2222_2026-09-24.diagnose). The IPv6 OpenSSH timeout persisted after that rollback. The Phase-5 candidate is therefore not the cause of this timeout.

Server tailscale IPv4/IPv6: 100.121.130.51 / fd7a:115c:a1e0::7a32:8233. Mac: 100.98.95.69 / fd7a:115c:a1e0::4132:5f45. The Mac route uses utun9; Tailscale ping and TSMP succeed. ICMPv6 and TCP 2222 time out. Tailscale userspace SSH and kernel OpenSSH take different paths.

The single authorized bounded header capture was completed at 2026-09-26 06:25:47–06:25:51 UTC, during one Mac socket connection attempt. It recorded five SYN transmissions/retransmissions for the same connection on tailscale0, no reply and no capture drops. Snap length was 60 bytes on the raw IP interface, with no payload-output options. No further capture was performed.

Privileged results:
- all.disable_ipv6=1; default.disable_ipv6=1; tailscale0.disable_ipv6=1; eth0.disable_ipv6=0.
- All inspected host Docker bridges/veth interfaces and lo have disable_ipv6=1.
- tailscale0 has no IPv6 address; there is no IPv6 Tailscale table-52 route/policy rule. Route lookup for the Mac IPv6 selects the public eth0 default route.
- OpenSSH listens on [::]:2222 and 0.0.0.0:2222. Effective configuration allows chatops only, prohibits root, enables public-key authentication and disables password/keyboard-interactive authentication (existing settings).
- IPv6 INPUT/FORWARD policies are DROP; there are no Tailscale IPv6 firewall chains. Terminal DROP counters did not increase across the capture snapshots. Concurrent background traffic prevents attributing every counter delta to this test.
- Journal reports ipv6=true but ipv6os=false.
- /etc/sysctl.conf persistently sets net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6 = 1; /etc/sysctl.d/99-sysctl.conf is its symlink. Forwarding is separately enabled.

Conclusion: the SYN reaches the server tunnel, but IPv6 is disabled on the receiving kernel interface and the expected local address/return path is absent. A missing listener or failure to reach the server is excluded. A firewall-only change cannot repair this state. Additional downstream firewall effects cannot be conclusively tested until the kernel IPv6 path exists.

## Correction safety gate — NO-GO

Linux documents that writing conf/all/disable_ipv6 changes default and every interface. Reading all=1 alone is not proof of IPv6 being disabled everywhere; eth0 is explicitly enabled here. The official Tailscale main-branch CheckIPv6 implementation nonetheless rejects all.disable_ipv6=1. The exact deployed 1.102.2 source revision could not be retrieved, so main-branch behavior is corroborating evidence, not an exact-version proof.

A bare all.disable_ipv6=0 would also enable IPv6 on currently disabled Docker bridges/veth devices. Restoring all=1 would also disable IPv6 on eth0 and remove affected addresses/routes. Reapplying only original scalar sysctls would not constitute a complete rollback. A tailscaled restart could additionally recreate IPv6 routing/firewall state and briefly affect the recovery path/Exit Node. Neither these effects nor a full state restoration have been validated offline.

Accordingly no sysctl, interface, route, firewall, daemon, container, Tailnet policy or provider rule was changed. No speculative static address/route workaround was applied. The authorization for a small reversible correction does not waive the required rollback proof or container/network isolation constraints.

Before activation: design and test a persistent per-interface-preserving initialization; establish exact deployed Tailscale IPv6 initialization behavior; back up addresses, routes, policy rules, firewall state, interface flags and affected files; prove rollback including public eth0 IPv6 and Tailscale-managed rules; stage and push the reviewed transaction and offline tests; retain the independent recovery console. Do not run sysctl -p or toggle the global flag as a shortcut.

